      },
      "lineNbr": 38,
      "author": {
        "id": 1892347
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-06T10:43:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hmm, it\u0027s been a while since I last looked at this. I looked over the C driver, and as far as I can tell, with one exception, all uses of local/internal refs just consider the sum of local and internal refs, so most of the time, it doesn\u0027t matter which kind of reference you use. The one exception is [this check](https://cs.android.com/android/kernel/superproject/+/common-android-mainline:common/drivers/android/binder.c;l\u003d991-999;drc\u003df955e6e98c348b65772fdc1db2a9eeac3a4c3fc8). It returns an error if a remote process tries to create a strong refcount, if there are no remote processes with a strong refcount (unless its the context manager). We already have a similar check in `NodeRef::clone` and `NodeRef::update`.\n\nInterestingly,  The driver uses local references in these two cases:\n\n* Refcounts held by transactions to the target process. (Both the target node, but also nodes embedded in the transaction.)\n* Pending BR_INCREFS and BR_ACQUIRE operations.\n\nIf you change the first category of references from local to internal references, then the two drivers actually store the same information, because the number of local references is now `active_inc_refs` field.\n\nThoughts?",
      "parentUuid": "c8ee6931_c3e5d074",
      "revId": "c0bf3401d8ad68c748aa0c67ef2ed634bafbfd7e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5c632633_24a69c68",
        "filename": "drivers/android/node.rs",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 38,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000205
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-08T17:58:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The most important difference between local and internal refs are that local refs are cleared (since they are no longer needed) when the process dies. Without that you will likely have a leak unless you have come up with a different solution to this than the C driver.",
      "parentUuid": "df9f9e82_6e15c487",
      "revId": "c0bf3401d8ad68c748aa0c67ef2ed634bafbfd7e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "399980f3_b6c1af82",
        "filename": "drivers/android/node.rs",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 38,
      "author": {
        "id": 1892347
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-09T09:45:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ultimately, the numbers tracked in the node don\u0027t control the lifetime of the node at all. That\u0027s tracked by the `Arc` pointer type that the node is wrapped in, and once all `Arc\u003cNode\u003e` references have gone out of scope, the node is deallocated even if the numbers in `NodeInner` are non-zero.\n\nIf we have a leak somewhere, then it\u0027s almost certainly due to a cycle of reference counted objects that keep each other alive. However, I\u0027ve tried to analyze the code for those in the past, and I have a piece of paper somewhere with all of the structs and arrows for the references, and I didn\u0027t find any cycles last time I did it. (Other than the cycles that `Process::deferred_release` will break, that is.)",
      "parentUuid": "5c632633_24a69c68",
      "revId": "c0bf3401d8ad68c748aa0c67ef2ed634bafbfd7e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3c8a66e2_6cff75db",
        "filename": "drivers/android/node.rs",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 38,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000205
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-10T01:56:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In this scheme, if you send a one-way transaction that contains a node that you then drop all other references to, how do you keep that node alive until the transaction is delivered? Do you allocate separate objects to store `Arc\u003cNode\u003e` references for all the nodes in a transaction (I did not see this, but I have not looked at every patch in detail yet).",
      "parentUuid": "399980f3_b6c1af82",
      "revId": "c0bf3401d8ad68c748aa0c67ef2ed634bafbfd7e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5d55319b_8ddda737",
        "filename": "drivers/android/node.rs",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 38,
      "author": {
        "id": 1892347
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-10T10:08:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The logic for this is mostly in `allocation.rs`. The target node is kept alive by the `NodeRef` in `AllocationInfo` (which in turn has an `Arc\u003cNode\u003e`).\n\nAs for other nodes embedded in the transaction, those are currently kept alive by just incrementing the process\u0027s refcount to the node in its `ProcessNodeRefs` collection. When the `Allocation` object is dropped, it reads the translated buffer data to figure out which `NodeRef` objects to adjust in `ProcessNodeRefs`. However, I intend to change this logic to have the allocation store a `NodeRef` for each embedded node, because the current way of doing it is what lead to aosp/2849924.\n\nAlso, as I write this, I realize that the current design allows the driver to drop refcounts to nodes owned by transactions with `BC_RELEASE`. This could cause errors to get emitted when cleaning up transactions due to it adjusting refcounts of nodes that the process no longer have a reference to.\n\nChanging it to just store a `NodeRef` for each embedded node would also solve this issue, because the transaction\u0027s refcount would be entirely separate from those that the process has. (I can allocate an array to store all of them, so I don\u0027t need separate allocations for each.)",
      "parentUuid": "3c8a66e2_6cff75db",
      "revId": "c0bf3401d8ad68c748aa0c67ef2ed634bafbfd7e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a64810af_022678ea",
        "filename": "drivers/android/node.rs",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 38,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000205
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-17T02:26:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e The logic for this is mostly in `allocation.rs`. The target node is kept alive by the `NodeRef` in `AllocationInfo` (which in turn has an `Arc\u003cNode\u003e`).\n\u003e \n\u003e As for other nodes embedded in the transaction, those are currently kept alive by just incrementing the process\u0027s refcount to the node in its `ProcessNodeRefs` collection. When the `Allocation` object is dropped, it reads the translated buffer data to figure out which `NodeRef` objects to adjust in `ProcessNodeRefs`. However, I intend to change this logic to have the allocation store a `NodeRef` for each embedded node, because the current way of doing it is what lead to aosp/2849924.\n\u003e \nRejecting transactions with overlapping offsets is needed regardless of how you keep track of the references. There was a bug on on this many years ago where a sender could crash or exploit libbinder in the receiving process by overwriting the pointer that it expected the driver to have validated.\n\n\u003e Also, as I write this, I realize that the current design allows the driver to drop refcounts to nodes owned by transactions with `BC_RELEASE`. This could cause errors to get emitted when cleaning up transactions due to it adjusting refcounts of nodes that the process no longer have a reference to.\n\u003e \n\u003e Changing it to just store a `NodeRef` for each embedded node would also solve this issue, because the transaction\u0027s refcount would be entirely separate from those that the process has. (I can allocate an array to store all of them, so I don\u0027t need separate allocations for each.)\n\nYou don\u0027t know how many NodeRefs you need until you have looped through the offset array though. Also note that the C version has internal and local reference counts only for the strong references. The internal weak references are kept track of with a list, not a count.",
      "parentUuid": "5d55319b_8ddda737",
      "revId": "c0bf3401d8ad68c748aa0c67ef2ed634bafbfd7e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
